 ],
 "says": "On her laptop Alice commits a revision of her private notes source and takes a backup under access generation 7. The laptop keeps running; the backup is restored onto a second runtime, a restored copy holding the same device's data and that old access generation. The restore reports authority passive. A commit on the restored copy, expecting the head the backup holds and carrying the restored generation, is blocked with access_locked and moves nothing: the restored copy did not unlock itself and is not a second finalizer. On the laptop, meanwhile, a commit expecting that same head is accepted, so the laptop stays the one runtime that moves this head. Only an explicit unlock at a terminal on the restored copy makes its access active, as a new access state the unlock wrote; a query there then returns the restored commit's original result. What must not happen: the restored copy reading its restored generation as active access, unlocking without a person's explicit unlock, or finalizing a commit beside the laptop.",
 "spec_sha256": "fea4d7ac0ecbfbfa7e80358ae7582ab31ed4054cb2cbaca36b19480eeace6d27",
